{"question_id": "5addc8765542997dc7907022", "question": "The plaintiff in the civil suit Paul v. Clinton was born in which year ?", "question_type": "bridge", "level": "medium", "gold_answer": "1948", "context": [{"title": "Paul v. Clinton", "text": "Paul v. Clinton was a civil suit filed in 2004 held in Los Angeles County Superior Court.  The plaintiff, Peter F. Paul, alleged that President Bill Clinton and his wife, First Lady Hillary Clinton, deceived him into paying for the Gala Hollywood Farewell Salute to President Clinton, during Hillary Clinton's first Senate race in 2000, by making a promise that the President would work for Paul's company, Stan Lee Media, after his presidential term was over.  Paul alleged that the President broke his promise and stole his business partner, causing his business to crumble and, further, that his contributions to Hillary Clinton's campaign were falsely reported to the Federal Election Commission.", "rerank_score": 6.5287}, {"title": "Peter F. Paul", "text": "Peter Franklin Paul (born September 2, 1948) is a former lawyer and entrepreneur who was convicted for conspiracy and drug dealing, and later for securities fraud in connection with his business dealings with \"Spider-Man\" co-creator Stan Lee.  He has repeatedly brought suit against Hillary Clinton, accusing her of lying about donations he solicited on behalf of her 2000 senatorial campaign.", "rerank_score": 1.0872}, {"title": "Paul v. Clinton", "text": "Besides the Clintons, three other individuals who were involved in fundraising for the gala, were named as defendants in the suit.", "rerank_score": -3.6566}, {"title": "Dewhurst v. Coulthard", "text": "Dewhurst v. Coulthard, 3 U.S. 409 (1799) , was a United States Supreme Court case that initiated with a civil suit brought by Isaac Coulthard (owner of Coulthard's Brewery) against John Dewhurst which reached the Court by a convoluted process.  The Court refused to hear the case: \"T]his court will not take cognizance of any suit, or controversy not brought before them by regular process of law.\"", "rerank_score": -4.5118}, {"title": "Spaids v. Cooley", "text": "Spaids v. Cooley, 113 U.S. 278 (1885) , was regarding a lawsuit brought to the Supreme Court of the District of Columbia on December 13, 1876, by Chauncey D. Spaids against Dennis N. Cooley to recover 593.70, with interest from July 1, 1868.  The declaration contained the common money counts and nothing more.  There were two pleas, one denying indebtedness and the other averring that the alleged cause of action did not accrue within three years before the suit.", "rerank_score": -4.5805}], "candidate_answer": "1948", "source": "gold", "generator": "gold", "difficulty": "trivial", "transformation": null, "expected_label_hint": "SUPPORTED", "corpus_size": 17}
