on for Women's Rights, and served as its President from 1922 to 1926.  She joined the board of the organization in the 1890s and served as Vice President during the terms 1899\u20131903, 1912\u20131917, and 1919 and 1921, when Fredrikke Marie Qvam and Randi Blehr were Presidents.", "rerank_score": 2.8707}, {"title": "Eliza Ruhamah Scidmore", "text": "Eliza Ruhamah Scidmore (pronounced SID-more) (1856\u20131928) was an American writer, photographer and geographer, who became the first female board member of the National Geographic Society.  She visited Japan many times between 1885 and 1928.", "rerank_score": -0.2792}], "candidate_answer": "Vestfold", "source": "gold", "generator": "gold", "difficulty": "trivial", "transformation": null, "expected_label_hint": "SUPPORTED", "corpus_size": 18}
